description.describe_eh:
This course that is designed to help students develop their understanding of real-world HR issues, and knowledge of international approaches to International Human Resource Management. Topics include: Recruitment (global and local resourcing); Training and Development: Developing Global Leaders and Expatriates; Global Performance Management; Total Rewards in the International Context; The Transfer of Employment Practices across Borders in Multinationals; People Management in Cross-Border Mergers and Acquisitions; Equal Opportunity, Diversity and Inclusion in the Global Context; and Corporate Social Responsibility and Sustainability through Ethical HRM Practices.
This course will help students:
?	demonstrate an understanding of key terms, theories/concepts and practices within the field of HRM;
?	obtain an in-depth knowledge of specific HRM-related theories, skills and practices;
?	appreciate the implications of increasing globalisation for the management of human resources, with particular reference to HRM in multinational corporations;
?	identify and appreciate the significance of ethical issues in HR practices and the management of people in the workplace.
In addition to there being a strong integration of recent research evidence and theory in this course (e.g. from top SSCI journals), students will be encouraged to reflect on how this knowledge can be applied by managers within organizations. Throughout the course, students will develop skills in interpreting work-related problems and issues, whilst analysing how organizations and employees can benefit from understanding recent evidence and knowledge in the field of international human resource management. The assessment will encourage students to discuss the application of theory and research evidence to the workplace.
